Chamfers: the end of a walked chamfer at a vertex where the face at the
end is tangent to both its faces (mitcad#263).

The case: a plate whose outline turns at a crease from a straight side
into an arc, its rim rounded along both, the crease chamfered from the
bottom to the top over both roundings. At the top the chain's edge (where
the roundings meet) ends at a vertex whose two other edges, A and Eq, join
the straight side's rounding Fa and the arc's rounding Fb tangentially to
the top Fv. The walk reaches that vertex with its contact on Fa at A and
its other contact inside Fb. The corner by intersection with Fv found
nothing, Fv being tangent to the chamfer's faces, and the general corner
(PerformMoreThreeCorner) threw in its projection: the chamfer failed.

ChFi3d_ChamferEndOnTangentFace (after WalkedCutToEnd, for chamfers only)
closes such an end with a ruled piece from the end section to the point Q
of Eq as far from the vertex as the section's point on A: its side on Fv
runs from that point to Q, its side on Fb from the section's point to Q,
each along a line of its face's parameters (their middles inside the
faces, the chords between them inside the material). The stripe ends in Q,
a free boundary with a singular extremity as WalkedCutToEnd's; ChFi3d_FilDS
cuts the edge such an end lies on also where the end has a degenerated
edge, both contacts on that edge.

